</figure>

<div class="col">
  <h2 id="contents">Contents</h2>
  <ol class="seclist"><li><h3><a href="maintenance-guides.html">Maintenance</a></h3><p>Oil changes on older engines, points and condenser basics, carburetor care, brake inspection, and the cooling system — plus a printable maintenance interval table.</p></li><li><h3><a href="restoration-planning.html">Restoration Planning</a></h3><p>Assess rust honestly, document a car properly, source parts responsibly, and plan a project that actually reaches the finish.</p></li><li><h3><a href="parts-and-tools.html">Parts &amp; Tools</a></h3><p>The essential garage tools, the manuals worth owning, the safety gear that matters, and how to organize a working shop.</p></li><li><h3><a href="classic-car-history.html">Car History</a></h3><p>The engineering milestones that shaped the automobile — the V8, the automatic transmission, and the rise of safety features.</p></li><li><h3><a href="garage-trivia.html">Garage Trivia</a></h3><p>Myth-busting and fun facts from the workshop, with the answers tucked just out of sight.</p></li></ol>

  <h2 id="who-for">Who this manual is for</h2>
  <p>The Torque Ledger is written for a broad fellowship of classic-car people. It is for the hands-on owner who wants to do their own routine work and understand their car deeply. It is for the newcomer who has just bought a first classic and feels a little daunted by its older ways. And it is for the owner who leaves the spanners to a professional but wants to understand what is being done, so they can be an informed customer and a better caretaker.</p>
  <p>Whatever kind of owner you are, we assume no expert knowledge and explain each term as it arises. We also keep a steady eye on comfort and safety for readers of every age, favoring the methods that are kinder to the back and the hands, and never urging anyone past what is safe for them to attempt. The goal is confident, well-informed ownership, whatever your level of involvement under the hood.</p>

  <h2 id="how-to-use">How to use this manual</h2>
  <p>This is a reference work, meant to be dipped into rather than read cover to cover. Each section stands on its own, with a contents index down the side so you can jump straight to the part you need, exactly as you would with a proper workshop manual. Where a job has real risks, you will find a clearly marked safety note; please read it before you begin. And throughout, we remind you of the single most important rule of classic-car work: when in doubt, consult the correct factory manual for your car and a qualified mechanic.</p>
  <p>We write for the thoughtful owner of any age, and we have taken care to make the pages easy on the eyes — large type that grows larger at a tap, clear diagrams in words, and procedures broken into simple numbered steps. You can print any section for the workbench, where a paper copy beats a screen with oily hands every time. Keep a printed copy of the maintenance interval table in the glovebox, and you will always have your schedule to hand.</p>

  <h2 id="why-manual">Why we made a manual, not a magazine</h2>
  <p>There are many fine places to admire classic cars from a distance. The Torque Ledger is for the owner who wants to roll up their sleeves, or at least to understand what is happening when someone else does. We chose the form of a manual deliberately: organized, methodical, and practical, the kind of reference you keep on the shelf and reach for when a job needs doing or a question needs answering.</p>
  <p>That does not mean it is dry. The best service manuals have a quiet character of their own, and we hope ours does too — plain-spoken, patient, and genuinely on your side. We believe that the knowledge to care for one of these machines should be freely available, clearly written, and safe, and that an owner armed with understanding gets far more joy from the hobby than one who feels at the mercy of it. That conviction is why the Ledger exists.</p>

  <h2 id="philosophy">The Ledger's philosophy</h2>
  <p>Our approach is captured in a few plain principles. <strong>Safety before everything:</strong> no repair is worth an injury, and some jobs belong to a professional, full stop. <strong>Method over haste:</strong> label your parts, keep your workspace tidy, and work in a logical order. <strong>Records always:</strong> write down what you do, when, and why, because a well-documented car is a well-cared-for car. And <strong>respect the machine:</strong> these are older vehicles that reward understanding and patience far more than force — a seized bolt freed gently is a victory, while the same bolt snapped off in haste is an afternoon lost.</p>
  <p>The Torque Ledger is an independent classic-car maintenance and restoration manual. It is free to read, sells nothing, and recommends no brands — when we name a make or part type, it is only to explain. The Torque Ledger is not affiliated with any manufacturer.</p>

  <h2 id="safety-foremost">Safety comes first, always</h2>
  <p>Before you turn a single bolt, let us be plain about the most important thing in this whole manual: your safety matters more than any repair. Classic cars are heavy, and they involve fuel, electricity, pressurized cooling systems, and tons of metal that must sometimes be lifted off the ground. Treated with respect, they are a joy to work on; treated carelessly, they can cause serious harm.</p>
  <p>So we will repeat a few rules throughout, and we make no apology for it. Never get under a car supported only by a jack — always use rated jack stands on firm ground. Never run an engine in a closed space. Never open a hot radiator. Keep fuel away from sparks, wear eye protection, and let hot parts cool. And above all, know your limits: brakes, fuel systems, and anything that leaves you uncertain belong to a qualified professional. There is no shame in that call; it is the mark of a wise owner. Pull up the hood and begin wherever your car needs you; the <a href="maintenance-guides.html">Maintenance</a> section is the usual first stop.</p>
